  "title": "Chapter 13 – Basic Mechanical Concepts",
  "order": 12,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "dl941i0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Force is an interaction that changes or tends to change the state of rest or uniform motion of a body (F = ma).",
        "Work is done only when a force produces displacement in the direction of the force (W = F × d × cos θ).",
        "Power represents the rate of doing work or energy consumption per unit time (P = W / t).",
        "Simple machines modify the magnitude or direction of an applied force to facilitate mechanical work.",
        "Mechanical Advantage (MA) is the ratio of output load to input effort, defining machine efficacy.",
        "Friction acts tangentially to oppose relative motion between contacting surfaces and can be minimized using lubricants and bearings."
      ],
      "id": "877doxr"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Work & Energy",
          "value": "Joule (J)",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
